---
import Doc from "../layouts/Doc.astro";
import ConceptMap from "../components/ConceptMap.astro";
import Doodle from "../components/Doodle.astro";
import { withBase } from "../lib/base";
---

<Doc
  title="The map"
  current="map"
  description="The whole field on one page: what machine learning is a branch of, and what branches off it."
>
  <div class="opener">
    <div>
      <h1>The map</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A course has to pick an order to teach things in, and any order hides
        the real shape of the subject. This page shows the shape.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="opener__art"><Doodle name="compass" /></div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Have a look at it now. Most of it won&rsquo;t mean anything yet, and that is
    expected. It&rsquo;s here so that each new idea has somewhere to go as it
    arrives, rather than turning up on its own with nothing around it. Come back
    every few chapters and watch it fill in.
  </p>

  <p>
    Three separate things hang off <em>machine learning</em>, and mixing them up
    is the most common source of early confusion. They are not three items on one
    list &mdash; they answer three different questions, and every project answers
    all three at once.
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>What feedback do you get?</strong> Supervised, unsupervised, self-supervised, reinforcement. This decides what data you have to go and collect, which is the expensive, hard-to-reverse decision.</li>
    <li><strong>What shape is the learned function?</strong> Linear model, tree, neural network. This decides how accurate you can get and how much computing it will take.</li>
    <li><strong>What makes the fitting work?</strong> Losses, gradients, backpropagation, validation. You need all of it whatever you picked above.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    So &ldquo;supervised learning with gradient-boosted trees, tuned against a
    validation split&rdquo; is one answer from each column. Not one thing with a
    long name — three small decisions wearing a trench coat.
  </p>

  <p>
    Once you can hear the three separately, a lot of the jargon stops being
    jargon.
  </p>

  <ConceptMap />

  <p>
    Solid boxes are taught here and link to their chapter. Dashed boxes are
    real things, in use somewhere today, and deliberately left out &mdash; the
    <em>nice to know</em> tier. Hover any box for a one-line description.
  </p>

  <h2>What this map is not</h2>
  <p>
    It isn't a history, and it isn't a ranking. A dashed box like <em>Gaussian
    processes</em> is not a lesser idea than a solid one — it's an idea a
    fortnight can't reach without cutting something better spent.
  </p>
  <p>
    The reason to draw them anyway is that you <em>will</em> meet the names, and
    knowing <em>where a name lives</em> is most of what it takes to read a paper
    without drowning. Several of the dashed boxes have a full piece waiting in the{" "}
    <a href={withBase("/extras/")}>extras</a> when you're curious.
  </p>

  <div class="pip-note">
    <Doodle name="pipThink" decorative />
    <p>
      A habit worth keeping for good: when you meet something new, find where it
      sits on this map. Almost everything is a new supervision signal, a new model
      family, or a new piece of the fitting machinery.
    </p>
  </div>
</Doc>
